<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Bojador</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape Bojador is a headland on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>. Its shoals, currents and winds made medieval European sailors fear that ships could not return from beyond it. In 1434 the Portuguese navigator Gil Eanes, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, rounded it and came back, opening the way for Portuguese exploration of the West African coast.</p></div>
